behaviour: |-
  **`PolicyServiceImpl.getPolicyDetails(id)` returns FULL policy JSON for ANY authenticated user — no role-based scoping, no permission gate.** `PolicyController.java:30` (`policyService.getPolicyDetails(policyId)`) handles `GET /api/policies/{policy_id}` with NO `SecurityRule` entry — falls through to the catch-all `pathMatchers("/**").authenticated()`. The list endpoint at `PolicyServiceImpl.list` (lines 52-60) DOES branch by role (admin gets paged repository result; non-admin gets in-memory filter of own-role-attached policies) — but `getPolicyDetails` does NOT apply any role-based filter. A non-admin user with a valid policy id can fetch ANY policy's full JSON statements, learning which permissions are bundled into which role. This is a CONFIDENTIALITY ISSUE: privileged Management/All policies are enumerable by id from any authenticated user. An attacker probing ids `1..N` reads every policy's statements; combined with the list-endpoint's role-based filter, the attacker can find policies they DO NOT have access to by id but learns their CONTENT. The disclosed information enables (a) building an attack model of how permissions are bundled; (b) targeting users to social-engineer into roles attached to specific policies.
